<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chernigov</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chernigov, now Chernihiv, is a historic city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, one of the oldest towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truce-of-deulino">Truce of Deulino</a> in 1618 confirmed Polish possession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> and Chernigov. This marked the maximum territorial achievement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> against Muscovy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chernigov.jpg" alt="Chernigov" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Valerii Sorokin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%A2%D1%80%D0%BE%D1%97%D1%86%D1%8C%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%BC%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B8%D1%80.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wars with the Grand Duchy of Lithuania in 1487–1494 and 1500–1503, including the victory at the Battle of Vedrosha in 1500, won the <b>Chernigov</b> and Seversk lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Towns grew rapidly: Kiev, Novgorod, <b>Chernigov</b>, Smolensk, Polotsk and Vladimir became centres of crafts and commerce, and Kiev under Yaroslav the Wise was one of the largest cities in Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Truce of Deulino of 1618, which confirmed Polish possession of Smolensk and <b>Chernigov</b>, marked the greatest territorial gains of Commonwealth arms in the east.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>On 10 January 1826 the <b>Chernigov</b> Regiment, led by Sergey Muravyov-Apostol, rose and captured Vasylkiv, but it was defeated within five days and over 3,000 people were arrested.</blockquote><a href="/movement/decembrist-revolt">Decembrist revolt · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Yaroslav the Wise assigned his sons principalities in order of seniority: Kiev and Novgorod to the eldest, Iziaslav; <b>Chernigov</b> and Tmutarakan to Sviatoslav Yaroslavich; Pereyaslavl and Rostov-Suzdal to Vsevolod; Smolensk to Vyacheslav and Volhynia to Igor.</blockquote><a href="/societies/kievan-rus">Social Hierarchy of Kievan Rus'</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Mongols destroyed <b>Chernigov</b> in 1239 and Prince Mikhail was killed in 1246, his son Roman Mikhailovich made Bryansk his seat, and it became the leading principality of the old Chernigov lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-bryansk">Principality of Bryansk</a></li></ul></section>
